    <mathmoddb:P984>https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q6674041</mathmoddb:P984>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q6821327">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q68663"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Dubinin–Astakhov isotherm model</rdfs:label>
    <schema:description xml:lang="en">micropore filling adsorption model with variable heterogeneity</schema:description>
    <mathmoddb:P558 xml:lang="en">https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q5729941</mathmoddb:P558>
    <mathmoddb:P1459 xml:lang="en">The Dubinin–Astakhov isotherm model is an empirical adsorption model developed to describe adsorption in microporous solids using a pore-filling mechanism. It generalizes the Dubinin–Radushkevich model by introducing a (flexible) exponent that allows flexible representation of surface heterogeneity and adsorption energy distributions. While originally proposed for heterogeneous microporous systems, experimental evidence shows that it can also successfully describe adsorption in nearly homogeneous microporous materials. The model expresses adsorption as a function of adsorption potential rather than concentration alone, making it explicitly temperature dependent. It is widely applied in adsorption studies of activated carbons, zeolites, and environmental adsorption processes.</mathmoddb:P1459>
    <mathmoddb:P1684 xml:lang="en">https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q6821128</mathmoddb:P1684>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q6821334">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q68663"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Frumkin isotherm model</rdfs:label>
    <schema:description xml:lang="en">adsorption isotherm with lateral adsorbate interactions</schema:description>
    <mathmoddb:P1459 xml:lang="en">The Frumkin isotherm model is an adsorption model that extends the Langmuir framework by explicitly accounting for lateral interactions between adsorbed molecules. It assumes monolayer adsorption on a surface with energetically equivalent sites while allowing attractive or repulsive interactions among neighboring adsorbates. The strength and sign of these interactions influence the shape of the adsorption isotherm and the variation of adsorption energy with surface coverage. In the absence of lateral interactions, the Frumkin model reduces to the Langmuir isotherm. The model is closely related to the Fowler–Guggenheim isotherm and is widely applied in solution-phase adsorption studies, including chemical wastewater treatment.</mathmoddb:P1459>
    <mathmoddb:P1684 xml:lang="en">https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q6775766</mathmoddb:P1684>
  </rdf:Description>
